1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,740
This is Doing and Feeling, Episode 10, The Evidence.

2
00:00:04,040 --> 00:00:11,660
In 2008, a team led by the neuroscientist Beatrice de Gelder reported a remarkable experiment and filmed it.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:20,600
Their subject, known as T.N., was a physician who had suffered two strokes that destroyed the primary visual cortex on both sides of his brain.

4
00:00:20,740 --> 00:00:26,040
By every clinical test, he was blind. He walked with a cane and said he saw nothing at all.

5
00:00:26,530 --> 00:00:30,620
The researchers set up a long corridor cluttered with boxes and a chair,

6
00:00:30,860 --> 00:00:33,640
took away his cane, and asked him to walk down it.

7
00:00:33,840 --> 00:00:39,060
He did, weaving carefully around every obstacle, while insisting he could not see them.

8
00:00:39,280 --> 00:00:44,240
That is the split between doing and feeling from episode one in a hospital corridor.

9
00:00:44,720 --> 00:00:49,340
Something in his brain was processing visual information well enough to steer his body.

10
00:00:49,660 --> 00:00:52,580
Yet, as far as he could tell, none of it was seen.

11
00:00:53,100 --> 00:00:57,520
Two episodes ago, we met a novel that imagined a whole species built that way.

12
00:00:57,780 --> 00:00:59,940
Here was one man for one sense.

13
00:01:00,200 --> 00:01:05,920
This episode is about evidence, what the science of consciousness can actually measure, and how far to trust it.

14
00:01:06,080 --> 00:01:10,300
The methods, the patience, the hidden awareness of people who seem unresponsive,

15
00:01:10,500 --> 00:01:16,200
the closest thing we have to a consciousness meter, the states that switch experience off and on, and the animals.

16
00:01:16,680 --> 00:01:21,840
Along the way, we'll find that some of the field's most famous results are shakier than their reputations.

17
00:01:22,580 --> 00:01:29,140
Theories wait until next episode. Today is data. The modern program began in 1990,

18
00:01:29,580 --> 00:01:34,580
when Francis Crick and Christophe Koch proposed setting the philosophical puzzle aside for a

19
00:01:34,580 --> 00:01:40,180
while and hunting for the neural correlates of consciousness. David Chalmers later gave

20
00:01:40,180 --> 00:01:45,540
the careful definition. A neural correlate of a conscious state is a minimal system whose

21
00:01:45,540 --> 00:01:51,540
activity is sufficient, under the right background conditions, for that state. The core method is

22
00:01:52,580 --> 00:01:54,140
called contrastive analysis.

23
00:01:54,520 --> 00:01:56,200
Hold the stimulus constant,

24
00:01:56,560 --> 00:01:58,700
arrange for it to be consciously perceived

25
00:01:58,700 --> 00:02:00,840
on some trials and not on others,

26
00:02:01,060 --> 00:02:03,400
and compare brain activity between the two.

27
00:02:03,660 --> 00:02:06,340
Whatever differs is a candidate correlate.

28
00:02:06,700 --> 00:02:07,840
But there's a trap,

29
00:02:07,880 --> 00:02:11,060
and much of the last 15 years has been about escaping it.

30
00:02:11,220 --> 00:02:14,320
Whatever differs between seen and unseen trials

31
00:02:14,320 --> 00:02:16,920
includes far more than the experience itself.

32
00:02:17,180 --> 00:02:19,100
It includes prerequisites,

33
00:02:19,260 --> 00:02:22,080
like attention, expectation, and arousal,

34
00:02:22,580 --> 00:02:24,720
which must be in place before you can see anything.

35
00:02:24,720 --> 00:02:28,180
And it includes consequences, the act of reporting,

36
00:02:28,420 --> 00:02:31,160
the memory of having seen, your confidence about it.

37
00:02:31,440 --> 00:02:34,340
In 2012, Jan Aru and colleagues,

38
00:02:34,560 --> 00:02:36,920
and separately Tom de Groff and colleagues,

39
00:02:37,120 --> 00:02:38,840
made this argument forcefully.

40
00:02:39,040 --> 00:02:42,520
A naive contrast picks up the machinery around consciousness

41
00:02:42,520 --> 00:02:44,300
along with consciousness itself.

42
00:02:44,680 --> 00:02:47,680
Every machine learning person knows that problem.

43
00:02:47,900 --> 00:02:51,460
If your probe cannot separate a property from its correlates,

44
00:02:52,580 --> 00:02:58,040
correlates and declare victory. Exactly, and the field's response has been to invent clever

45
00:02:58,040 --> 00:03:04,700
contrasts. Here is the toolkit, because these methods are the experimental heart of the field.

46
00:03:05,280 --> 00:03:10,260
Masking, flash an image for a few hundredths of a second, then replace it with a pattern.

47
00:03:10,720 --> 00:03:16,500
Set the timing at threshold, and the very same flash is seen on some trials and missed on others.

48
00:03:17,160 --> 00:03:18,100
Binocular rivalry.

49
00:03:18,400 --> 00:03:22,600
Show each eye a different image, say a face to one and a house to the other.

50
00:03:22,960 --> 00:03:28,560
Instead of a blend, your experience alternates every few seconds, while the input never changes.

51
00:03:29,540 --> 00:03:35,340
Nikos Lagathetes and his colleagues trained monkeys to report which image they were seeing during rivalry

52
00:03:35,340 --> 00:03:38,200
and recorded neurons along the visual pathway.

53
00:03:38,460 --> 00:03:43,500
In the earliest visual areas, only a small minority of cells followed the reported percept.

54
00:03:43,500 --> 00:03:46,940
In an intermediate area, a bit more than a third did.

55
00:03:47,160 --> 00:03:51,980
In the high-level face and object areas of the temporal lobe, about 9 in 10 did.

56
00:03:52,380 --> 00:03:57,540
In those single neurons, awareness seemed to be reflected late in the hierarchy, not early.

57
00:03:58,060 --> 00:04:03,820
Later brain imaging in humans, though, found percept-linked signals even in primary visual cortex.

58
00:04:04,460 --> 00:04:10,200
Continuous flash suppression, introduced by Naotsu Gutsu-chiya and Koch in 2005,

59
00:04:10,600 --> 00:04:12,940
is rivalry turned up to full strength.

60
00:04:13,500 --> 00:04:19,120
Flash rapidly changing colorful patterns to one eye, and a static picture shown to the other eye,

61
00:04:19,320 --> 00:04:24,420
can stay invisible for many seconds, long enough to test what the brain does with things you never

62
00:04:24,420 --> 00:04:31,380
see. Inattentional blindness. In Daniel Simons and Christopher Chabris's famous video, people

63
00:04:31,380 --> 00:04:37,060
counting basketball passes often fail to notice a person in a gorilla suit strolling through the

64
00:04:37,060 --> 00:04:43,240
scene. Then one of the most important methodological innovations of recent years, no-report paradigms.

65
00:04:43,500 --> 00:04:48,500
If report contaminates the contrast, infer what someone is experiencing without asking.

66
00:04:48,880 --> 00:04:54,480
During rivalry, the eyes make involuntary tracking movements that follow whichever image is dominant.

67
00:04:54,940 --> 00:05:03,800
In 2014, Stefan Fressler and colleagues used those movements, along with pupil size, to read out the percept with no report at all.

68
00:05:04,020 --> 00:05:12,080
Frontal activity linked to rivalry was reduced when report was removed, but not abolished, and some frontal regions stayed active.

69
00:05:13,500 --> 00:05:19,220
also changes how people attend, so even this cleaner contrast is not perfectly clean.

70
00:05:19,380 --> 00:05:24,760
That result is ammunition for both sides of the front versus back debate next episode.

71
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:31,880
And in October 2026, Doris Tso's lab, which had already used a no-report design with single face

72
00:05:31,880 --> 00:05:37,880
cells, posted a preprint based on recordings from thousands of neurons at once. Two monkeys

73
00:05:37,880 --> 00:05:43,700
passively watched displays in which one face was flashed and another suppressed. In face-selective

74
00:05:43,700 --> 00:05:48,580
regions, activity flipped back and forth between representing the seen face and the suppressed one,

75
00:05:48,920 --> 00:05:55,200
each state lasting roughly 60 milliseconds. It's two animals from one lab, not yet peer-reviewed,

76
00:05:55,300 --> 00:06:00,980
and the face counted as seen was the one human viewers reported. A switching rate is not a frame

77
00:06:00,980 --> 00:06:06,500
rate for experience, but it hints that what you see may be a rapidly contested state rather than

78
00:06:08,220 --> 00:06:11,820
Now a measurement problem that sounds technical and is not.

79
00:06:12,020 --> 00:06:14,160
How do you know someone did not see something?

80
00:06:14,420 --> 00:06:16,900
The obvious answer is that they told you.

81
00:06:17,120 --> 00:06:19,220
But a report depends on two things,

82
00:06:19,220 --> 00:06:21,280
how much information gets through

83
00:06:21,280 --> 00:06:23,780
and how willing a person is to say yes.

84
00:06:24,240 --> 00:06:27,580
Signal detection theory, borrowed from radar engineering,

85
00:06:27,980 --> 00:06:28,660
separates them.

86
00:06:29,100 --> 00:06:32,060
Sensitivity is how well you can tell signal from noise.

87
00:06:32,660 --> 00:06:35,880
Criterion is how much evidence you demand before saying,

88
00:06:36,080 --> 00:06:36,880
I saw it.

89
00:06:37,880 --> 00:06:44,580
A cautious observer with a strict criterion will say nothing was there on many trials where some faint experience was present.

90
00:06:44,920 --> 00:06:53,260
This is the criterion problem, and in 2021, the philosopher Ian Phillips used it to challenge one of the fields found in cases.

91
00:06:54,020 --> 00:06:58,020
Blindsight, he argued, may not be vision without experience at all.

92
00:06:58,180 --> 00:07:06,940
It may be degraded conscious vision, so faint and strange that patients, applying the standard of normal sight, sincerely report seeing nothing.

93
00:07:07,880 --> 00:07:16,180
On that reading, blindsight shows how unreliable a report of nothing can be, rather than showing that sophisticated vision can run in the dark.

94
00:07:16,440 --> 00:07:22,160
So the most famous evidence for doing without feeling might be feeling that's too weak to count.

95
00:07:22,520 --> 00:07:27,760
That's the live question, and worth holding in mind for every patient case in this episode.

96
00:07:28,400 --> 00:07:35,920
Defenders of the classic reading point to signal detection studies suggesting that blindsight differs from faint, normal vision.

97
00:07:38,140 --> 00:07:44,360
but it has pushed the field to measure confidence and response bias far more routinely than it once did.

98
00:07:45,320 --> 00:07:47,600
With that caution in hand, the patients.

99
00:07:48,200 --> 00:07:50,680
Blindsight was named by Lawrence Weisskrantz,

100
00:07:50,800 --> 00:07:55,780
whose patient DB had part of his primary visual cortex surgically removed.

101
00:07:56,300 --> 00:08:00,260
DB denied seeing anything in the affected half of his visual field,

102
00:08:00,440 --> 00:08:03,680
yet when forced to guess, he could point to objects there

103
00:08:03,680 --> 00:08:07,400
and discriminate orientation and movement far above chance.

104
00:08:07,880 --> 00:08:13,940
Weisskrantz and colleagues' key report appeared in 1974 and his monograph in 1986.

105
00:08:14,460 --> 00:08:18,040
Some blindsight patients report no experience at all,

106
00:08:18,340 --> 00:08:20,640
others a vague feeling that something is there.

107
00:08:20,880 --> 00:08:22,720
And there's effective blindsight.

108
00:08:23,160 --> 00:08:29,140
Patients respond, above chance, to the emotional expressions of faces they say they cannot see.

109
00:08:29,760 --> 00:08:32,320
Hemispatial neglect is in some ways stranger.

110
00:08:32,660 --> 00:08:35,720
After damage typically to the right parietal lobe,

111
00:08:35,720 --> 00:08:42,960
patients ignore the left side of space. In 1978, Edordo Bisioc and Claudio Lutzati

112
00:08:42,960 --> 00:08:48,420
asked neglect patients in Milan to imagine standing in the cathedral square and describe

113
00:08:48,420 --> 00:08:54,500
what they saw. They described only the buildings on their imagined right. Asked to imagine standing

114
00:08:54,500 --> 00:08:59,640
at the opposite end of the square, they described the other side and omitted what they had just

115
00:08:59,640 --> 00:09:05,640
described. The neglect lived in the representation of space itself. Then there's the most famous

116
00:09:05,720 --> 00:09:11,940
neglect case of all. In 1988, John Marshall and Peter Halligan reported a patient shown two

117
00:09:11,940 --> 00:09:16,820
drawings of a house, identical, except that one had flames coming from its left side.

118
00:09:17,060 --> 00:09:21,840
She said the drawings were the same. Yet when asked which house she would rather live in,

119
00:09:22,040 --> 00:09:27,460
she mostly chose the one that was not burning. It became a textbook example of unconscious

120
00:09:27,460 --> 00:09:32,720
information guiding choice. Here's what the textbooks often leave out. It was a single

121
00:09:32,720 --> 00:09:40,320
patient. A 1990 follow-up by Bisioc and Maria Luiser-Rosconi got inconsistent results. And in

122
00:09:40,320 --> 00:09:47,500
2025, a pre-registered replication tested 54 stroke patients, 21 of them with neglect. The

123
00:09:47,500 --> 00:09:53,720
burning house effect appeared in just one patient, who did not have neglect. Exploratory analyzes

124
00:09:53,720 --> 00:09:59,480
suggested that when people did show a preference, they preferred intact drawings over merely shaded

125
00:09:59,480 --> 00:10:05,540
ones, so the effect may not have been about fire at all. One patient and possibly about shading,

126
00:10:05,840 --> 00:10:12,440
not fire. How many textbook cases rest on one patient? More than you'd like. Others have held

127
00:10:12,440 --> 00:10:18,840
up well, like the imagined paesa. The lesson is to weigh a famous single case by how often it has

128
00:10:18,840 --> 00:10:24,400
been reproduced, rather than by how often it has been retold. Then the split-brain patients.

129
00:10:24,760 --> 00:10:28,780
To treat severe epilepsy, some patients had the corpus callosum,

130
00:10:28,960 --> 00:10:31,900
the main bridge between the hemispheres, surgically cut.

131
00:10:32,360 --> 00:10:35,720
Roger Sperry, who shared a Nobel Prize for this work,

132
00:10:35,880 --> 00:10:40,740
and Michael Gazzaniga showed that each hemisphere could respond to information the other did not have.

133
00:10:41,060 --> 00:10:43,400
In Gazzaniga's most famous demonstration,

134
00:10:43,820 --> 00:10:46,320
the speaking left hemisphere saw a chicken claw,

135
00:10:46,500 --> 00:10:48,980
and the right hemisphere saw a snowy scene.

136
00:10:49,120 --> 00:10:51,100
The right hand picked a picture of a chicken,

137
00:10:51,100 --> 00:10:54,840
and the left hand, controlled by the right hemisphere, picked a shovel.

138
00:10:55,340 --> 00:11:00,200
Asked why, the left hemisphere, which never saw the snow, answered without hesitation.

139
00:11:00,500 --> 00:11:04,640
The claw goes with the chicken, and you need a shovel to clean out the chicken shed.

140
00:11:05,240 --> 00:11:08,420
Gazzaniga called the system behind such answers the interpreter,

141
00:11:08,740 --> 00:11:15,200
a process that generates fluent, confident explanations for behavior whose real causes it cannot see.

142
00:11:15,480 --> 00:11:21,000
For anyone who works with language models, that's the most important result in this episode.

143
00:11:21,100 --> 00:11:30,340
An articulate explanation of a system's own behavior tells you nothing, on its own, about whether the explanation is causally accurate.

144
00:11:30,620 --> 00:11:39,760
That's the parallel drawn in Episode 6, where a model described carrying digits it never carried and will hold onto it for Episodes 12 and 13.

145
00:11:39,960 --> 00:11:42,580
And it is not confined to split brains.

146
00:11:42,580 --> 00:11:47,880
In Choice Blindness Experiments, the next entry in Episode 8's File on Introspection,

147
00:11:48,480 --> 00:11:53,380
Petter Johansson and Lars Hall had people choose which of two faces they found more attractive,

148
00:11:53,720 --> 00:11:58,080
then secretly handed them the other photo and asked them to explain their choice.

149
00:11:58,520 --> 00:12:02,620
On most of the manipulated trials, people did not notice the swap

150
00:12:02,620 --> 00:12:06,360
and explained why they had preferred a face they had actually rejected.

151
00:12:06,720 --> 00:12:09,920
Even the split brain itself is less settled than it looks.

152
00:12:10,380 --> 00:12:14,500
In 2017, Yer Pinto and colleagues reported two patients

153
00:12:14,500 --> 00:12:18,440
who could respond to stimuli anywhere in the visual field with either hand,

154
00:12:18,580 --> 00:12:21,500
though they could not compare stimuli across the two halves,

155
00:12:21,680 --> 00:12:26,500
and suggested that perception may be split while the person remains one conscious agent.

156
00:12:26,900 --> 00:12:29,460
With two patients, that is contested.

157
00:12:30,260 --> 00:12:35,480
The most consequential findings concern patients who seem completely unresponsive.

158
00:12:35,740 --> 00:12:37,300
Clinically, there's a spectrum.

159
00:12:37,740 --> 00:12:40,220
In coma, patients cannot be roused.

160
00:12:40,440 --> 00:12:44,840
In the vegetative state, now often called unresponsive wakefulness syndrome,

161
00:12:45,220 --> 00:12:49,580
they have sleep-wake cycles and open their eyes, but show no sign of awareness.

162
00:12:49,880 --> 00:12:54,660
In the minimally conscious state, signs of awareness are inconsistent but genuine.

163
00:12:55,220 --> 00:12:57,320
Bedside diagnosis is hard.

164
00:12:57,520 --> 00:13:01,980
In a well-known 2009 study using a careful standardized scale,

165
00:13:02,320 --> 00:13:07,200
18 of 44 patients diagnosed as vegetative by clinical consensus

166
00:13:07,300 --> 00:13:14,580
about 41 percent turned out to be minimally conscious. In 2006, Adrian Owen and colleagues

167
00:13:14,580 --> 00:13:21,320
published a short paper in Science that changed the field. A 23-year-old woman, five months after

168
00:13:21,320 --> 00:13:27,500
a traffic accident, met every criterion for the vegetative state. In an FMR eye scanner,

169
00:13:27,800 --> 00:13:33,240
she was asked to imagine playing tennis and then to imagine walking through the rooms of her house.

170
00:13:33,240 --> 00:13:38,280
Her brain activity matched that of healthy volunteers, motor planning areas for tennis,

171
00:13:38,740 --> 00:13:40,680
spatial navigation areas for the house.

172
00:13:40,880 --> 00:13:42,600
She was following instructions.

173
00:13:42,940 --> 00:13:47,560
It was a single patient, and critics wondered whether the words alone triggered automatic

174
00:13:47,560 --> 00:13:48,160
responses.

175
00:13:48,500 --> 00:13:54,600
But in 2010, a larger study found that 5 of 54 patients could modulate their brain activity

176
00:13:54,600 --> 00:14:00,160
this way, and one could answer yes or no questions, imagining tennis for yes and the house for

177
00:14:00,160 --> 00:14:00,420
no.

178
00:14:00,980 --> 00:14:06,480
Then, in 2024, a study in the New England Journal of Medicine led by Yelena Bodian

179
00:14:06,480 --> 00:14:11,700
tested 353 patients at six centers in Europe and North America.

180
00:14:12,140 --> 00:14:17,700
Of the 241 who could not follow commands at the bedside, 60, a quarter,

181
00:14:17,980 --> 00:14:21,280
showed covert responses on brain imaging or E.

182
00:14:21,460 --> 00:14:25,580
E.g., the field calls this cognitive-motor dissociation.

183
00:14:25,780 --> 00:14:28,900
And among patients who could follow commands at the bedside,

184
00:14:30,420 --> 00:14:32,720
28% showed responses on the scans.

185
00:14:33,120 --> 00:14:38,960
The tests miss many people who are plainly aware, so a negative scan cannot rule awareness

186
00:14:38,960 --> 00:14:39,320
out.

187
00:14:39,560 --> 00:14:45,720
A quarter in this group, but following commands in a scanner is still access, in block sense.

188
00:14:46,120 --> 00:14:48,280
Does it tell us anything about experience?

189
00:14:48,680 --> 00:14:54,540
In a person, access is the best evidence of experience we ever get, and we grant it to

190
00:14:54,540 --> 00:14:55,760
each other on far less.

191
00:14:55,760 --> 00:15:02,140
So it raises urgent questions about pain, communication, and decisions to withdraw care.

192
00:15:02,320 --> 00:15:07,420
And it's the clearest demonstration that behavior can radically underestimate consciousness.

193
00:15:08,380 --> 00:15:13,040
Can we measure consciousness directly without asking the person to do anything?

194
00:15:13,280 --> 00:15:17,220
The closest thing we have is the Perturbational Complexity Index,

195
00:15:17,500 --> 00:15:23,760
developed by Marcello Massimini, Giulio Tononi, and colleagues, and first published in 2013.

196
00:15:25,760 --> 00:15:31,560
and zip. You zap the cortex with a pulse of transcranial magnetic stimulation and record

197
00:15:31,560 --> 00:15:38,500
the brain's echo with EEG. Then you zip it. You compress the spatial and temporal pattern of the

198
00:15:38,500 --> 00:15:44,020
response, using the same kind of algorithm that compresses computer files. A conscious brain

199
00:15:44,020 --> 00:15:49,280
answers a perturbation in a way that is both integrated, spreading widely, and differentiated,

200
00:15:49,500 --> 00:15:55,260
producing a complex, hard-to-compress pattern. An unconscious brain responds either locally,

201
00:15:55,760 --> 00:15:57,140
like a stone dropped in mud,

202
00:15:57,320 --> 00:16:00,400
or with a big stereotyped wave, like a bell ringing.

203
00:16:00,920 --> 00:16:03,480
Either way, the response compresses easily.

204
00:16:03,740 --> 00:16:06,700
In the first study, values in dreamless sleep

205
00:16:06,700 --> 00:16:10,060
and under anesthesia fell roughly between 0.1 and 0.3,

206
00:16:10,300 --> 00:16:13,560
and wakefulness ran from about 0.4 to 0.7.

207
00:16:14,280 --> 00:16:15,980
Vegetative patients were low,

208
00:16:16,340 --> 00:16:18,340
minimally conscious patients intermediate

209
00:16:18,340 --> 00:16:19,820
and locked in patients,

210
00:16:20,020 --> 00:16:23,140
fully conscious but paralyzed, in the waking range.

211
00:16:23,140 --> 00:16:36,520
In 2016, Sylvia Cacerado and colleagues calibrated a cutoff of 0.31 that separated conscious from unconscious conditions in a benchmark group of 150 people without a single error.

212
00:16:37,060 --> 00:16:45,620
Applied to 43 vegetative patients, it flagged 9 with high complexity, and 6 of those 9 later recovered signs of awareness.

213
00:16:45,920 --> 00:16:48,900
The most striking calibration case is ketamine.

214
00:16:49,400 --> 00:16:53,360
At anesthetic doses, it leaves people completely unresponsive.

215
00:16:53,480 --> 00:16:59,720
But they often report vivid dreams afterward, and under ketamine, the complexity index stayed high,

216
00:16:59,940 --> 00:17:04,200
matching what people later reported rather than how they looked from the outside.

217
00:17:04,680 --> 00:17:09,940
Notice where the measure's credibility comes from, though. It's calibrated against report.

218
00:17:10,220 --> 00:17:18,100
Right. Every consciousness meter is ultimately validated against people who can tell us, afterward, whether they experienced anything.

219
00:17:18,900 --> 00:17:23,500
That's fine for humans. It becomes the deep problem for machines in episode 12.

220
00:17:24,520 --> 00:17:29,480
Some of the most powerful evidence comes from switching consciousness off and back on.

221
00:17:29,820 --> 00:17:35,460
General anesthesia is the only routine procedure that reliably interrupts experience and then

222
00:17:35,460 --> 00:17:41,580
returns it. But unresponsive is not the same as unconscious. There's an old, simple trick

223
00:17:41,580 --> 00:17:47,200
called the isolated forearm technique. Before the muscle relaxant is given, a tourniquet is

224
00:17:48,900 --> 00:17:56,080
that hand can still move. In a 2017 study across six hospitals, 260 patients were asked, after

225
00:17:56,080 --> 00:18:02,060
intubation, to squeeze with that hand. Twelve of them, about four and a half percent, responded to

226
00:18:02,060 --> 00:18:07,840
the command. None of those questioned afterward remembered anything. So for some patients, under

227
00:18:07,840 --> 00:18:13,580
some regimes, there is connected awareness without memory, and the absence of a memory is not proof

228
00:18:13,580 --> 00:18:18,700
that nothing was experienced. Anesthetics act through quite different molecular mechanisms,

229
00:18:18,900 --> 00:18:24,080
yet the ones that abolish experience seem to share a breakdown of integrated communication

230
00:18:24,080 --> 00:18:30,640
across the brain. In September 2026, a study led by Andrea Luppy reported a common signature

231
00:18:30,640 --> 00:18:38,240
of anesthesia in six species—humans, macaques, marmosets, mice, zebrafish, and nematode worms.

232
00:18:38,440 --> 00:18:44,300
The drugs and recording methods differed. But in every species, activity in different parts

233
00:18:44,300 --> 00:18:49,720
of the nervous system became less coordinated, and its past predicted its future less well.

234
00:18:50,080 --> 00:18:56,040
Deep structures matter too. In 2020, Michelle Redinbaugh and colleagues found that stimulating

235
00:18:56,040 --> 00:19:01,840
a small region of the central thalamus could wake anesthetized macaques while the anesthetic was

236
00:19:01,840 --> 00:19:08,820
still flowing. And in 2025, deep electrodes in five human patients showed certain thalamic nuclei

237
00:19:08,820 --> 00:19:13,960
becoming active before the prefrontal cortex on trials where a stimulus was consciously seen,

238
00:19:14,300 --> 00:19:18,680
as if the thalamus were a gate. Sleep makes the same point from another angle.

239
00:19:18,860 --> 00:19:25,300
You're not unconscious all night. You dream, and not only in REM sleep. In 2017, Francesca

240
00:19:25,300 --> 00:19:30,180
Sicleri, Tononi, and colleagues woke people repeatedly through the night and asked whether

241
00:19:30,180 --> 00:19:36,220
they had been experiencing anything. Dreaming, in both REM and non-REM sleep, went with less

242
00:19:36,220 --> 00:19:41,940
slow-wave activity and more fast activity in a posterior region of the cortex. In a follow-up

243
00:19:41,940 --> 00:19:48,020
with seven sleepers, that signal alone predicted in real time during non-REM sleep whether they

244
00:19:48,020 --> 00:19:54,540
would report a dream with about 87% accuracy. We'll meet that posterior hot zone again next

245
00:19:54,540 --> 00:20:01,560
episode. And in 2021, four labs reported two-way communication with lucid dreamers who solved

246
00:20:01,560 --> 00:20:06,560
simple arithmetic problems during REM sleep and signaled their answers with eye movements.

247
00:20:07,240 --> 00:20:09,480
Psychedelics push in yet another direction.

248
00:20:09,840 --> 00:20:13,320
Robin Carhart-Harris' entropic brain hypothesis

249
00:20:13,320 --> 00:20:16,940
and the model he developed with Carl Friston called Rebus

250
00:20:16,940 --> 00:20:21,320
proposed that psychedelics relax the brain's high-level prior beliefs,

251
00:20:21,560 --> 00:20:24,080
letting bottom-up signals flow more freely.

252
00:20:24,260 --> 00:20:27,680
That connects directly to the predictive brain of episode 5

253
00:20:27,680 --> 00:20:30,940
and fits the finding that the diversity of brain signals,

254
00:20:31,120 --> 00:20:33,480
a cousin of ZAP and ZIP's compression measure,

255
00:20:33,480 --> 00:20:41,500
rises under psilocybin, LSD, and ketamine. In 2024, a study in Nature by Joshua Segel and

256
00:20:41,500 --> 00:20:47,160
colleagues scanned people repeatedly before, during, and after a dose of psilocybin. The

257
00:20:47,160 --> 00:20:52,120
drug desynchronized brain networks more than three times as much as a stimulant control,

258
00:20:52,400 --> 00:20:57,740
most strongly in the default mode network, the system most associated with self-referential

259
00:20:57,740 --> 00:21:02,940
thought, and reduced coupling between the hippocampus and that network persisted for weeks.

260
00:21:03,900 --> 00:21:09,120
Then there's a source of evidence that is very old and newly scientific, meditation.

261
00:21:09,940 --> 00:21:14,580
Contemplative traditions have been mapping states of mind for more than 2,000 years.

262
00:21:14,940 --> 00:21:21,180
The Mandukya Upanishad distinguishes waking, dreaming, and dreamless sleep from a fourth

263
00:21:21,180 --> 00:21:26,560
state it describes as pure awareness. Buddhist traditions describe meditative absorptions

264
00:21:26,560 --> 00:21:32,060
called jhanas and a state called Nirodha Samapati, the attainment of cessation.

265
00:21:33,480 --> 00:21:38,900
first-person claims made by people who have trained their attention for decades. On their own,

266
00:21:39,100 --> 00:21:44,660
they're not data in the scientist's sense, but they generate hypotheses that can now be tested.

267
00:21:44,980 --> 00:21:51,840
The philosopher Thomas Metzinger's 2024 book, The Elephant and the Blind, analyzes more than 500

268
00:21:51,840 --> 00:21:57,640
reports from meditators of what he calls minimal phenomenal experience, awareness with little or

269
00:21:57,640 --> 00:22:04,140
no content, and no sense of self or time. If that state exists, it's a precious test case for any

270
00:22:04,140 --> 00:22:10,920
theory that ties consciousness to self-modeling or to rich content. And in 2023, Ruben Lachonen,

271
00:22:11,200 --> 00:22:16,200
Matthew Sackett, Helene Slogter, and colleagues set out a research program on meditative cessations.

272
00:22:16,640 --> 00:22:21,280
Brief, apparently complete gaps in consciousness that some advanced practitioners say they can

273
00:22:21,280 --> 00:22:26,940
induce at will, and much longer ones in the traditional accounts. Early EEG work has studied

274
00:22:27,640 --> 00:22:33,640
advanced meditator intensively across repeated cessation events. All of this is early, small,

275
00:22:33,840 --> 00:22:40,540
and dependent on trained reports. Trained reports are still reports. Why trust a meditator more than

276
00:22:40,540 --> 00:22:46,960
the shoppers of Episode 8? On reports alone, you should not, and every caveat about introspection

277
00:22:46,960 --> 00:22:53,120
from Episode 8 applies. What training buys is consistency and the chance to pair reports with

278
00:22:53,120 --> 00:22:59,700
measurements. That's Varela's neurophenomenology from last episode, finally getting data. And if

279
00:22:59,700 --> 00:23:05,020
consciousness can be switched off by training as well as by drugs, that's an experimental handle

280
00:23:05,020 --> 00:23:11,840
no anesthesiologist has. And any complete theory has to account for the extremes. The cessations,

281
00:23:12,180 --> 00:23:18,940
the dreams under ketamine, the awareness without content. The free will experiments deserve care

282
00:23:18,940 --> 00:23:21,280
because they're so often summarized wrongly.

283
00:23:21,440 --> 00:23:26,740
In 1983, Benjamin Libet asked people to flex their wrist whenever they felt like it,

284
00:23:26,860 --> 00:23:30,980
and to note, using a fast-moving clock when they first felt the urge.

285
00:23:31,160 --> 00:23:35,080
A slow buildup of electrical activity, the readiness potential,

286
00:23:35,480 --> 00:23:38,900
began a few hundred milliseconds before the reported moment of decision.

287
00:23:39,340 --> 00:23:40,860
The headline wrote itself,

288
00:23:41,160 --> 00:23:42,980
Your Brain Decides Before You Do.

289
00:23:43,360 --> 00:23:45,400
Three later findings complicate it.

290
00:23:45,400 --> 00:23:51,780
In 2012, Aaron Scherger, Jacobo Sitt, and Stanislas DeHaan showed that the readiness

291
00:23:51,780 --> 00:23:56,860
potential can be explained as random neural noise drifting toward a threshold, with the

292
00:23:56,860 --> 00:24:01,780
movement launched when it crosses. Averaged backward from the moment of movement, that

293
00:24:01,780 --> 00:24:06,860
noise looks like a ramp. On this reading, the early signal is not a hidden decision at all.

294
00:24:07,220 --> 00:24:13,700
In 2008, Chun Xiangsoon, John Dylan Haynes, and colleagues decoded which of two buttons

295
00:24:15,400 --> 00:24:22,440
RI activity several seconds in advance, but with accuracy only around 60%, where chance is 50.

296
00:24:22,640 --> 00:24:29,320
And in 2019, Yuri Maos and colleagues had people decide which of two charities would receive a real

297
00:24:29,320 --> 00:24:34,820
thousand-dollar donation. The readiness potential was present when the choice was arbitrary,

298
00:24:35,100 --> 00:24:41,000
and absent or much reduced when it was deliberate. So these experiments concern the timing of

299
00:24:41,000 --> 00:24:47,100
arbitrary movements. They're interesting about how actions get started. On their own, they fall

300
00:24:47,100 --> 00:24:53,960
far short of refuting free will in the senses philosophers usually debate. Here's a question

301
00:24:53,960 --> 00:24:59,020
that connects back to the first part of the series. If machines can do so much without

302
00:24:59,020 --> 00:25:05,780
consciousness, how much can humans do without it? Less than the hype suggested. The famous 1957

303
00:25:05,780 --> 00:25:10,200
claimed that flashing the words eat popcorn in a cinema-boosted sales was a hoax.

304
00:25:10,420 --> 00:25:15,560
In 1962, its author admitted it had been a gimmick, with too little data to mean anything.

305
00:25:16,240 --> 00:25:20,720
Unconscious thought theory, which claimed that complex decisions go better when you're distracted,

306
00:25:21,100 --> 00:25:27,060
has largely failed to replicate. Masked priming is real, but in careful studies its effects are

307
00:25:27,060 --> 00:25:32,420
small and short-lived. The picture that has survived is roughly this. Unconscious processing

308
00:25:32,420 --> 00:25:38,920
in humans is fast, parallel, and specialized, and can extract some meaning from words. But flexible,

309
00:25:39,360 --> 00:25:43,940
multi-step reasoning and acting on novel combinations seem to depend on conscious

310
00:25:43,940 --> 00:25:50,240
access. Holding information over time mostly does too, though some studies find faint traces of

311
00:25:50,240 --> 00:25:56,440
unseen items lasting a few seconds. Which is interesting, because in machines that coupling

312
00:25:56,440 --> 00:26:03,080
apparently does not hold. Language models do multi-step reasoning, and most researchers doubt

313
00:26:03,080 --> 00:26:09,000
their conscious. That's one of the deepest puzzles where our two topics meet. Either human brains

314
00:26:09,000 --> 00:26:14,440
couple flexible reasoning with consciousness for contingent architectural reasons, in which case

315
00:26:14,440 --> 00:26:20,440
machines show the coupling is optional. Or machines that reason flexibly have something functionally

316
00:26:20,440 --> 00:26:27,100
like conscious access, whether or not anything is experienced. Episodes 12 and 13 take both

317
00:26:27,100 --> 00:26:35,500
readings seriously. Finally, animals. In 2020, Andreas Nieder and colleagues trained two carrion

318
00:26:35,500 --> 00:26:41,360
crows to report whether they had seen a faint stimulus. Neurons in a high-level region of the

319
00:26:41,360 --> 00:26:46,680
crow forebrain tracked stimulus intensity at first, but during the delay before the response,

320
00:26:46,680 --> 00:26:52,440
they tracked what the crow went on to report, including its false alarms. That's a neural

321
00:26:52,440 --> 00:26:59,660
correlate of subjective report in a bird, whose brain, as we saw in episode 2, arrived at cortex-like

322
00:26:59,660 --> 00:27:06,160
circuits by a separate evolutionary route. In April 2024, a group of scientists and philosophers

323
00:27:06,160 --> 00:27:11,820
launched the New York Declaration on Animal Consciousness. It states that there is strong

324
00:27:11,820 --> 00:27:17,440
scientific support for attributing conscious experience to mammals and birds. It states that

325
00:27:17,440 --> 00:27:22,480
there is at least a realistic possibility of it in all vertebrates, and in many invertebrates,

326
00:27:22,680 --> 00:27:29,340
including at least cephalopods, decapod crustaceans, and insects. And it says that it is irresponsible

327
00:27:29,340 --> 00:27:35,600
to ignore that possibility in decisions affecting those animals. About 40 researchers signed at the

328
00:27:35,600 --> 00:27:40,760
launch, and hundreds more have signed since. The method behind statements like that is what the

329
00:27:41,820 --> 00:27:47,300
Birch calls a theory-light approach. Rather than commit to one theory, look for behavioral markers

330
00:27:47,300 --> 00:27:53,160
associated with conscious experience in humans. Do animals trade off avoiding pain against getting

331
00:27:53,160 --> 00:27:58,020
something they want? Do they tend their wounds or learn to avoid places where they were hurt?

332
00:27:58,380 --> 00:28:03,360
Hermit crabs, given small shocks, are more willing to abandon a poor shell than a good one.

333
00:28:03,560 --> 00:28:10,380
In 2021, Robin Crook gave octopuses a painful injection. They tended the spot, later avoided

334
00:28:11,820 --> 00:28:14,900
and preferred one where a local anesthetic had brought relief.

335
00:28:15,160 --> 00:28:18,540
The octopus from episode 2, it seems, can hurt,

336
00:28:18,720 --> 00:28:23,860
and the ball-rolling bumblebees from episode 2 met the behavioral criteria for play.

337
00:28:24,300 --> 00:28:29,200
Episode 14 returns to how evidence of this kind changed the law in Britain.

338
00:28:29,580 --> 00:28:31,660
And none of that transfers to AI.

339
00:28:32,340 --> 00:28:33,960
That's the point to end on.

340
00:28:34,300 --> 00:28:40,940
Every marker in this episode was calibrated in creatures that share our evolutionary history and our biology.

341
00:28:41,820 --> 00:28:44,800
In animals, behavior and biology come bundled,

342
00:28:44,820 --> 00:28:48,420
so a behavioral marker is evidence about the machinery underneath.

343
00:28:48,780 --> 00:28:52,200
A language model can produce any behavioral marker you can name,

344
00:28:52,660 --> 00:28:54,900
trade-off talk and pain talk included,

345
00:28:55,180 --> 00:28:58,760
because it learned from text written by creatures that have the machinery.

346
00:28:59,240 --> 00:29:02,500
For machines, theory-like approaches break down

347
00:29:02,500 --> 00:29:04,400
and were forced back onto theory,

348
00:29:04,640 --> 00:29:07,900
which is why the next episode puts the theories on trial.

